00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:07.910
Welcome!

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:10.760
- Well, hello, thank you
very much for having me.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.020
- Absolutely.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:16.270
So, I always like to first ask people,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.500
how did you end up studying
grading and grades?

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.450
What was your path to get here?

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.967
- Yeah, that's a really fun question,

00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:26.960
and one I'm really excited
and passionate about.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.450
I don't think I went into education,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.500
I don't think anyone
really went into education

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.170
to get better at grading.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:36.450
In fact, it might just be
something that we all have to do.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.900
Although in 2008, I was a
high school math teacher

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.110
here in Iowa, and I went

00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:42.230
to the state math teacher conference,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.360
and there was a breakout session.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.070
It was called how to fix
your broken grade book

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.347
led by another high school math teacher.

00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:48.117
And I thought, "Well, I'll just go

00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:49.797
"and see if I can take something away

00:02:49.797 --> 00:02:52.890
"back to my own classroom
and get a little bit better."

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.000
Kristen, literally that
45 to 50 minute session

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.600
changed my professional career forever.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.670
I came back really excited,

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.390
talked to our high school
principal at the time

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.570
and just thanked him for
allowing me to go back in 2008.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.697
And he just really encouraged me to say,

00:03:09.697 --> 00:03:11.897
"Hey, could you try out
something that you learned

00:03:11.897 --> 00:03:13.792
"from this session in your class?"

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:14.857
And I thought, "Well,
there's only nine weeks

00:03:14.857 --> 00:03:15.690
"left in the school year.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:16.640
"That seems kind of crazy."

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.230
But I picked my last period geometry class

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.257
and just started changing some
things up related to grading.

00:03:22.257 --> 00:03:23.890
And as a result of that, I started reading

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.640
every book or article or blog post

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.300
I could find about grading.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.000
Fast forward a couple years later,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.140
I had a number of conversations
with other teachers

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.710
in our high school and our middle school,

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.050
and they started thinking
differently about grading.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:36.490
Fast forward another year or so later,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.470
and I got promoted to the district office

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.250
and we had this dilemma in
our school in Solon, Iowa,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.170
this little rural community in Iowa,

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.280
where some of our teachers
were thinking differently

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.500
about grading and others weren't yet.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.700
And so we had a decision to make,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.650
were we going to kind of be
okay with that middle ground,

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.000
or were we going to go on a journey to see

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.260
if we all wanted to think
about grading differently.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.020
So obviously we made that to decision

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.640
to all think about grading differently,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.080
and so my experience comes
from being a classroom teacher,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.077
doing this standards-based grading thing,

00:04:06.077 --> 00:04:09.280
and also leading a change
in our school district

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.890
to make grading practices
significantly different.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.160
You mentioned our books,
"Making Grades Matter."

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.000
And again, as a high school math teacher,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.360
I don't really consider
myself to be an author

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.950
or really that great of a writer,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.230
but through others that have written books

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.470
and heard about our work,
we're actually encouraged

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.030
to write our own book, and so
in standards-based grading,

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.690
it's not always viewed
as something that is

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.400
easily attainable at the
secondary level, in particular.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.440
And so our book really helps

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.790
middle school and high school teachers

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.770
take their next steps with
standards-based grading.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.630
So that a little bit about my journey.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.780
Currently though, I work at
the University of Northern Iowa

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:46.260
where I teach future
principals and superintendents,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.258
and then also support as
many educators as possible

00:04:49.258 --> 00:04:50.690
to take their next steps

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.420
towards more effective grading practices.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:53.930
And it's just a thrill to be here today

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.660
talk about a topic I'm really, really,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.560
really passionate about.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:58.430
- That is great.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.773
So let's start at the beginning.

00:05:00.773 --> 00:05:03.160
What is standards-based grading?

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.270
- Yeah, great.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.260
Maybe the best way to explain it is

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:12.110
it's a philosophy or a
process or a set of practices

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.750
that really aim to communicate

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.880
students current levels of learning

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.660
separately from their behaviors.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.310
When you think about maybe
the grading practices

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.020
that you and I experienced as a student,

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.000
maybe we got like a 95% in a class,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.990
but that 95% could have mean
we got 95% of the points

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.870
or it could have meant we
actually got 100% of the points

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.740
and turned in a couple assignments late,

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.470
and so maybe in the past,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.920
we've mixed kind of
behaviors with learning.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.600
In standards-based grading,
those two things are separate.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.040
And I think about when I
take my car to a mechanic,

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.260
sometimes I do a 20-point inspection,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.490
and on that 20-point inspection,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.930
it tells me if my brakes are
really good or not very good,

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.340
if my battery is really
good or not very good,

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.180
and so the mechanic kind of breaks down

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:01.960
the components of my vehicle and gives me

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.000
very specific feedback on
how well my car is doing

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.850
in each one of those,
and so that's the aim

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.090
of standards-based grading,
is really just to give

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.110
our students a much more
detailed perspective

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.030
on a standard-by-standard
basis, if you will,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.560
on what they're doing really well at

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.930
and what they could get even better at.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.766
And also embeds elements

00:06:21.766 --> 00:06:23.570
of probably something I know
you're very passionate about,

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.486
mastery learning as well,

00:06:25.486 --> 00:06:27.790
and I hope we can talk
more about that today.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:28.623
- That's great.

00:06:28.623 --> 00:06:31.450
So from a student perspective,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.900
what are the benefits of grading this way,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.910
as opposed to a more traditional way?

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.320
- Yeah, so maybe in the past
students have found themselves

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.130
not really knowing what the
currency of the classroom is.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.320
Sometimes it's just all about earning

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:49.680
when they're at that cusp of 89.4%

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.930
and they want to get their
grade up to an 89.5%,

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.420
so it'll get rounded up to
the favorable grade they want

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.820
to get the scholarship or the honor roll

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.700
or the GPA that they want.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:00.627
They may just ask the teacher,

00:07:00.627 --> 00:07:03.440
"What can I do to improve my grade?"

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.740
And maybe the teacher is
in a good mood that day

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:08.030
and says, "Here's a extra
credit crossword puzzle,"

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:10.020
or "Here's something else you can do,"

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.100
but maybe the teacher's
not in that great of a mood

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.840
or just maybe has more of a, hey, you need

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.220
to just do better next time mindset

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.680
because the currency of the
classroom is all about earning

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:22.870
at that point.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.400
When the currency of the classroom,

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.330
which is what standards-based
grading is all about,

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.197
is more about learning,
now, a student might ask,

00:07:29.197 --> 00:07:32.187
"Hey, Mr. Townsley, I'm not very good yet

00:07:32.187 --> 00:07:34.017
"at Pythagorean's Theorem.

00:07:34.017 --> 00:07:35.527
"Could I show you

00:07:35.527 --> 00:07:38.410
"how I'm better at
Pythagorean's Theorem now?"

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.000
And by showing me as the teacher,

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.980
that they're actually better
at Pythagorean's Theorem,

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.280
then as a result of that,
their grade might improve,

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.330
which is a good thing, right,

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:49.080
because if a student has
learned more of the math stuff

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.210
or the science stuff or
the social studies stuff.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.620
They should of course have a better grade

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.380
because the purpose of grades

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.020
is really to communicate students'

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:57.890
current levels of learning.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.950
So students really, I believe, are more,

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.900
they have more ownership
in the learning process

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.020
because it's more about what's
what they're in control of,

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:09.000
which is the extent to
which they've learned

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.350
the standards in the class.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:12.593
- Got it.

00:08:12.593 --> 00:08:13.930
So I see that link.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.710
So as we think about
mastery learning here,

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.160
one of the things that
we're thinking about is

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.580
that you're doing whatever
the work you need is

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.710
to increase your proficiency
or work towards mastering.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.325
It's not that you get one shot at a test

00:08:27.325 --> 00:08:29.200
and that's your score,

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.670
but you can keep learning and keep trying

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.030
and improving on that over time.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.440
Does that fit in then to this idea

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.320
of grading based on standards?

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:40.547
- Yes, indeed, it does.

00:08:40.547 --> 00:08:41.710
And in my own research,

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:43.960
I've read quite a bit
about mastery learning,

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:47.700
so I can confirm this
on that level, Kristin.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.680
As I think back to mastery learning,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.230
I think of Benjamin Bloom and others

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.940
that have really set the foundation

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.170
for what mastery learning is.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.470
And back in the day,
there was this mentality

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.400
of, well only a certain
percent of the students

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.000
ought to deserve an A or only
a certain element of students

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.960
ought to be successful in the class,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.253
kind of that bell shaped
curve, IQ testing mentality.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.360
But when Bloom others really
challenged that mindset,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.057
they said, "You know what?

00:09:13.057 --> 00:09:17.597
"If we provide students more
time and the right conditions,

00:09:17.597 --> 00:09:19.987
"many, many, many, many more students

00:09:19.987 --> 00:09:21.750
"can learn at a high level."

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.190
In other words, if
achievement can be constant

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.120
and time can become the variable,

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.460
then even more students
can learn at a high level.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.020
And as I think about
standards-based grading,

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.080
that's really what it aim to do.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.750
As I think about the
grading practices, again,

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.750
that maybe you and I experienced
back in the day, Kristen,

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.050
if we did not do very
well kind of early on

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.340
in the learning process,
maybe we turned in

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.640
some homework assignments that
we didn't really understand

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:47.950
what was going on in our class,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.000
we kind of dug ourselves into a hole

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.400
point-wise or percentage-wise.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.230
Within a standards-based grading mindset,

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:58.260
it's really all about how
much has a student learned,

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.210
and if Kristen, if you or
I are in the same class

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.550
and you may learn it before I do,

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:06.280
one of the premises of
standards-based grading

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.810
is it doesn't really matter
when a student learns it.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.430
We honor the fact that students learn

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.690
at different rates and different paces.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.430
And if my learning comes later,

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.730
I'm gonna erase that
old version of learning

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:18.900
in the grade book, and
I'm gonna replace it

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:21.070
with more recent evidence of learning.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.116
And again, that's maybe a
mindset shift in particular

00:10:24.116 --> 00:10:26.150
for many of our secondary
educators out there,

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.120
but it really comes.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.490
It's not necessarily a new idea.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.070
It comes from years and
years and years of theory

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.970
and research related to mastery learning.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:37.550
I think Leanne Young and Tom
Gusty in their 2011 article

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.730
in the Clearinghouse really
made some strong connections

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:43.190
between mastery learning
and also the response

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:44.840
to intervention model that many schools

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.280
are working towards right now

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:47.247
that just says, "Hey, you know what?

00:10:47.247 --> 00:10:49.867
"If we could really provide
quality instruction,

00:10:49.867 --> 00:10:51.687
"if we can also then
provide the right amount

00:10:51.687 --> 00:10:53.437
"of interventions to students who still

00:10:53.437 --> 00:10:56.587
"do not yet understand it, we
could help a lot of students

00:10:56.587 --> 00:10:57.700
"learn at a high level."

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.550
And all of those things really fit in

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.620
the standards-based grading
and mastery learning bucket,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:03.453
if you will.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:05.510
- That totally makes sense.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.950
And this also is reminding me of links

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:11.190
to motivation research, where there's,

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:14.070
whether students are motivated to learn,

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:17.280
or whether they're
motivated to get a score

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.250
that shows what they learn
and that students who are

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.690
motivated based on learning a new thing

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:26.010
tend to be more engaged and
more interested in learning.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.732
Is that connected to all of this, as well,

00:11:28.732 --> 00:11:30.810
in terms of increasing student motivation?

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:31.643
- Of course, yes.

00:11:31.643 --> 00:11:33.820
Again, as I think about
the default feedback

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.870
that I included, that I gave to students

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.760
back in the day before I went to this

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.700
state math teacher conference,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:43.370
I would frequently give
students like a 14 outta a 16

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.040
on a quiz, but that 14 out of 16

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.130
really didn't tell the student

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:48.227
what they're doing really well at

00:11:48.227 --> 00:11:50.160
and what they need to get better at.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.920
I mean, imagine if I went
to the doctor's office,

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.830
the doctor's office said,
"You're about a 85% health."

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.987
I'd be like, "Well,
what's the other 15%, doc?

00:11:57.987 --> 00:11:59.140
"Tell me, tell me, tell me!"

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:02.850
And so at the heart of
standards-based grading is using

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:04.920
some other type of scale.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:09.423
Many schools have used an almost,

00:12:09.423 --> 00:12:12.521
or a proficient or some
other descriptor of learning

00:12:12.521 --> 00:12:16.410
to tell a student exactly
the extent to which they have

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.340
learned that particular standard.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.870
And really what it is, it's
much more specific feedback

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.540
to the student on exactly
what they're doing

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.890
and what they need to do to get better.

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:28.040
One of the things that I look back

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.400
on in my own teaching career and wonder is

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.070
how often was I giving
feedback to students

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:37.213
that really helped them
understand how to close the gap.

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:41.730
And so sometimes when
educators start doing this

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:42.830
standards-based grading thing,

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.920
they realize that it's
not just the grade book

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:46.960
that needs to improve,

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:50.160
but it's also their feedback practices.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.460
And if I could just give a
shout out to so many wonderful

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.320
researchers and authors out there,

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.120
Susan Brookhart, Dylan William, and others

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.750
that have really talked about
feedback being at the heart

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:02.430
of the learning process

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:05.260
and our students really deserve feedback,

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:09.610
not on a unit three test or
in just an essay perspective,

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.280
but on a much more granular level,

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:14.033
on a standard-by-standard level.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:18.140
And we provide 'em that very
detailed level of feedback,

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:18.973
you're right, Kristen.

00:13:18.973 --> 00:13:21.040
The research suggests
they're much more excited,

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.293
motivated to improve upon that feedback.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:25.600
- That makes sense.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.950
So you said this could be a
change in thinking and mindset

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:32.600
for some educators in where things are.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.060
What are some things that,

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:37.140
for instance, when you came
back from this conference,

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:41.160
what are maybe some initial
steps that teachers can take

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.660
towards this different mindset?

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:45.920
- Yeah, a couple things.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.430
One is, in my mind I tend
to think about textbooks

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:54.600
and chapters as being the
driving force for what I taught.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:57.630
In fact, I'll never forget
when got my first teaching job,

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.300
I was assigned to teach geometry
and several other courses.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.097
And I asked some of
their teachers and said,

00:14:02.097 --> 00:14:04.850
"Hey, what are kids supposed
to learn in geometry?"

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:06.467
And they said, "Well, here's the textbook.

00:14:06.467 --> 00:14:08.157
"Most years we get to chapter 12

00:14:08.157 --> 00:14:10.187
"and some years we get to chapter 13.

00:14:10.187 --> 00:14:11.380
"Here you go."

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:13.930
And so of course that's what I did,

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.950
but now what I know is you most states

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:20.210
have some list of state standards, right?

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.670
There have been some people
that have gotten together

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:24.190
and said these are the
most important things

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:26.420
that students are supposed
to learn in biology

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:29.790
or algebra one or seventh grade science.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.210
And so instead of thinking
about the textbook

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:34.570
being the driving force in what we teach,

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.800
we should look at the standards.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.870
I worked with some PE teachers
even in our school district.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.557
And they said, "Well, I was
just so used to always doing

00:14:42.557 --> 00:14:46.097
"the volleyball unit or the
PE or the football unit,"

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.127
and frankly that's the way
they've always done it,

00:14:49.127 --> 00:14:51.300
and so that's okay.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.100
But we started thinking about what are

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.740
the specific learning goals.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.090
What are the specific
standards that students

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.190
are supposed to be learning in PE

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:00.680
or seventh grade science or in math?

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.980
And so, instead of thinking
about the activities

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:04.600
and the units as being the driver,

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:06.730
we have to first start
thinking about the standards

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.510
or the learning goals being the driver.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:10.650
And then secondly, we
have to design assessments

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.040
that are very well aligned
to those learning goals

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:14.280
or those standards.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.010
Again, so many of us we've just inherited

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:18.223
the assessments, right?

00:15:19.411 --> 00:15:22.220
The tests that come with
the textbook materials

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.590
or the tests or the
quizzes or the projects

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.213
that have been handed down
from one teacher to the next.

00:15:28.213 --> 00:15:30.790
We have to ask ourselves
to what extent are those

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:34.260
actually aligned to the
learning goals that we want.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:37.700
And then finally, if I'm thinking

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.560
about just a typical high
school or middle school teacher,

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:41.190
so many of us are used to grading

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.780
based upon points and percentages,

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:46.580
and points and percentages
don't really do the best job

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:50.110
of communicating to students
what they're really good at

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:51.820
and what they're not really good at.

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:52.653
I mean, think about this.

00:15:52.653 --> 00:15:53.940
What's the difference between a student

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.940
who has 96% on a test and 94% on a test?

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.260
Is there some, is there a difference

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.620
that's actually describable?

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.580
Can we put our mind and our hands

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:10.430
and our fingers on what that
difference is in that 2%?

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.480
So when educators real
wrap their mind around

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:15.210
the standards-based grading
thing, they're going to,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:16.750
instead of thinking about percentages,

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:20.010
they're going to think about
some levels of learning,

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:23.283
beginning, developing, area
of concern and proficient.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.020
And again, that's just a huge
mind shift for so many of us,

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.930
not because we may not want to do it,

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:32.480
but because in our teacher
preparation programs,

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.350
we haven't necessarily been
taught how to do it yet.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:39.220
And I'm confident as we
continue to improve and evolve

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.320
in our teacher preparation

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:41.760
and administrative preparation programs,

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.110
that in time we'll get to that point

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.910
where more educators will be
familiar with this process.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.980
- Yeah, so that leads me to think

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.710
how much can a teacher
do this on their own,

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.910
versus how much do they
need the school support,

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.750
changing what the report cards look like,

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:01.890
changing what the parents
are expecting to come home

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:03.120
and where those are?

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.680
What's the balance between those?

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:07.630
- Oh, Kristen, you're giving
me some good flashbacks

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:12.030
to 2008, 2009 as myself and
just a few other teachers,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:12.930
as we started doing it.

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.070
Frankly, Kristen, it was an uphill battle

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:17.780
for a number of years
because in our classrooms,

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:21.150
we were trying to reculture,
as I referred to earlier,

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.290
as the currency of our
classroom for students

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:24.933
being all about learning.

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:29.610
And again, this is not
because the other teachers

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:31.110
from our school were consciously doing it,

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:33.910
but that's just because
that's just what they knew.

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:36.410
And so students in my class,

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:39.750
I might've been the only teacher
that they had all day long,

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:43.250
where the currency of the
classroom was learning early on.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:45.720
And so it takes a really passionate,

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.907
knowledgeable teacher
to say, "You know what?

00:17:47.907 --> 00:17:50.367
"There's going to be some hiccups.

00:17:50.367 --> 00:17:52.447
"There are going to be
some implementation dips

00:17:52.447 --> 00:17:53.761
"along the way."

00:17:53.761 --> 00:17:55.570
And one very specific one,
as we think about, again,

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:59.460
this idea of mastery
learning is that time becomes

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.763
the variable, not the constant.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:04.200
We want achievement to be the constant.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:06.350
So myself as a teacher,
I have to figure out

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:09.700
times during my class period
times during the school day

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:12.380
where I can provide additional
instruction to students

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.560
who have not yet
demonstrated an understanding

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.270
of Pythagorean's Theorem

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:18.670
or the other standards in the classroom.

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:22.650
And that's much easier when
the whole school is on board.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.220
We'll hear about schools
doing an intervention time

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.440
or a what I need time,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.710
or if their mascot is tiger a tiger time

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:34.610
where school-wide
students are provided time

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:37.860
for additional instruction
and reassessments.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.880
If those specific times during
the day are not in place,

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:43.940
and if the culture of most classrooms

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.980
is still more about earning
than it is about learning,

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:49.210
for individual teachers,
this is going to be

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:53.050
a pretty big uphill battle.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:55.050
And so that's why in our
book, "Making Grades Matter,"

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:58.270
we talk about the
individual classroom shifts

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.290
in many of our chapters
and we talk about doing it

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:05.090
as a team, as a professional
learning community together.

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:06.470
And then in the final chapter of our book,

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.830
we also talk about
specifically how to make

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:10.920
this entire shift as school.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:12.770
And so in the research that I've done

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:15.870
and that I've seen, Kristen,
almost always, there is

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:19.530
a certain level of an
implementation dip that happens.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.530
And it's most often because
we're not approaching it

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:24.230
as an entire school

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.660
and understanding that there
will be an implementation dip.

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:27.960
And so I appreciate that question.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:30.810
So I wanna encourage listeners
and viewers out there

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:32.684
that are really thinking
about making the shifts

00:19:32.684 --> 00:19:36.260
to know and appreciate
the implementation dip

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:41.040
and to ride it out and to
encourage our entire school

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.870
to make these changes in time.

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:44.943
And it really does take time.

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:47.820
- That totally makes sense.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:50.111
So we have a couple
questions that are similar

00:19:50.111 --> 00:19:52.050
to each other along the same line.

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:55.130
So one from Avive and
one from Lance McConnell.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:20:00.130
So what are the things to
start to work with teachers

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.320
to help shift mindsets and Avive asks

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.580
if teachers have less flexibility,

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.740
what are some things that they can do,

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:10.500
again, a first couple of steps

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.720
to start changing their practice?

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.590
- Yeah, I'd say a couple things.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:18.287
One is just to first take a
kind of a balcony view and say,

00:20:18.287 --> 00:20:21.600
"What's the purpose of
grades in my classroom?"

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.200
Sometimes we'll kind of jump right down

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.740
to the actionable things
that a teacher should do.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:30.760
And maybe instead of giving
zeros for incomplete work,

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:32.300
maybe just give fifties,

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.770
sometimes hear about schools doing that.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.480
And while that's an admirable practice,

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.030
in absence of really focusing

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.070
on what's the purpose of grades,

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:41.459
oh, the purpose of grades
is to communicate learning.

00:20:41.459 --> 00:20:43.360
So just having the
conversation with a department,

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.040
having a conversation with
an individual teacher,

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:46.630
having a conversation
at a faculty meeting,

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:48.740
like what do we believe
as a school or as a team

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.230
is the purpose of grading.

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:52.680
And then if, while we're
waiting for maybe everyone

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.620
to move forward in an actionable way

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:57.050
in some of the things I mentioned earlier,

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:59.540
individual teachers can
start doing what I call

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:03.410
having one foot in the
standards-based grading world,

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.913
and one foot in the
traditional grading world.

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:08.900
We can experiment with our students.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:12.350
For example, at an upcoming test,

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.410
if you know as a teacher
that you cannot yet

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:17.150
put like a one, two, three or four

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:19.404
or a area of concern, beginning,

00:21:19.404 --> 00:21:22.220
almost there, proficient
in the grade book yet,

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:25.510
you could still provide
that same level of feedback

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:28.460
to students on an upcoming test

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.450
for each standard or learning goal.

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:35.177
And just ask students, "Hey,
with this level of feedback,

00:21:35.177 --> 00:21:36.777
"what do you want to do next?

00:21:36.777 --> 00:21:39.297
"How can I help you as a teacher improve

00:21:39.297 --> 00:21:40.407
"on these learning goals?

00:21:40.407 --> 00:21:42.037
"What can you do as a student

00:21:42.037 --> 00:21:44.010
"to improve on these learning goals?"

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:45.460
And you can have the conversation

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:47.890
without having to change anything
at all in the grade book.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:50.520
Now, in that scenario, you would still put

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:53.110
whatever the building or
the district requires,

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.730
like a percentage in the grade book,

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:57.650
but you would have the
conversation with your students

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:00.691
to see how they would react
to a much more specific

00:22:00.691 --> 00:22:02.560
learning goal feedback.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.640
And I think what most educators will find

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:09.640
is that students actually crave
that same level of feedback.

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:11.430
One of the things I
did in my own classroom

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:12.760
is I started asking students

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.520
about the level of feedback they got

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:18.280
from their drama director
or their musical director,

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:21.150
or their band instructor,
or their football coach.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:25.210
And most often all of those
extracurricular or co-curricular

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:29.650
sponsors and coaches gave
them very detailed feedback.

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:32.150
And so I think if we make that connection,

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:34.260
even if we can't be all
in in the grade book

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:36.830
because of building or
district policy constraints,

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:39.920
we can still begin to provide
that same level of feedback

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:43.400
to our students and see
how they react to it.

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:44.700
And what that's gonna require

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.100
is just taking a more precise
look at our assessments

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:47.933
and allowing them the standards.

00:22:47.933 --> 00:22:52.050
And I think any teacher
can start doing that now

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:53.270
without having to have the permission

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:56.650
of their administration, or
have some switch turned on

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.500
at the electronic grade book
level to make that possible.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:02.330
- Great.

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:05.393
Here's another question
from Facebook, Greg Mullin.

00:23:06.255 --> 00:23:07.800
It's a little bit
different, but interesting.

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:10.390
How does standards-based grading

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.237
help prepare students
for higher education?

00:23:12.237 --> 00:23:14.930
And I wonder if there's a
sub-question in there of like,

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:16.240
if I have good grades like this

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:19.910
are college is gonna
look at it kinda funny?

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:22.120
- Yeah, fun to hear some of these names

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:23.200
gonna turn out there, Kristen.

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:24.033
Thank you, Lance.

00:23:24.033 --> 00:23:25.620
Thank you, Greg, for your questions.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.530
So regarding your question here, Greg,

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:28.750
just a fantastic question.

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:30.990
One that is very important to discuss,

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:33.950
specifically for high
schools making the shift.

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:38.890
First, so often secondary
parents or secondary teachers

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:39.927
or secondary administrators will say,

00:23:39.927 --> 00:23:42.407
"If we do this, is it really going

00:23:42.407 --> 00:23:45.010
"to prepare students for college?"

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:46.550
First, a couple things.

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:50.070
First, most of the high
schools I know that do this,

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:53.440
they still eventually determine
a specific letter grade

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:55.800
from the standard marks in the grade book.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:58.300
And so these high schools still have a GPA

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:02.420
that's still used for admissions
and scholarship decisions.

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:03.920
Now down in the weeds a little bit,

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:07.180
then a next question maybe
Greg's thinking about is,

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:08.640
but what if we provide students

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:10.380
these multiple opportunities?

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.410
What if we provide these redos and retakes

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:14.290
in the spirit of mastery learning,

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:16.980
they may or may not do
that at the college level?

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.240
Well, what we have to
know at the K12 level is

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:23.880
our aim, because of federal legislation,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:27.250
such as No Child Left behind
and Every Student Succeeds Act,

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:30.720
our aim is to help as many
kids as possible get it,

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.500
like that's our job in the K12 world.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.210
That may or may not be

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:38.550
what the university and
college folks are doing.

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:39.650
And again, as a college professor,

00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:41.300
I can kind of speak to that

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:43.510
that there's just a
different paradigm sometimes

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:44.820
at the college university level.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:49.170
So, regardless of what
college or universities do,

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:51.827
our aim in K12 is to help as
many kids as possible get it.

00:24:51.827 --> 00:24:54.570
And so we believe that kids
learn at different rates

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:55.990
and different paces.

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:58.860
So of course then it makes
sense to provide students

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:01.290
multiple opportunities to
demonstrate their learning

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:04.600
through this whole reteaching
and reassessment process.

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:07.260
And finally, some research that I've done

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:11.140
with Dr. Thomas Guskey
and Dr. Thomas Buckmiller

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:13.640
suggests that college students

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:17.060
at the end of their first
semester, freshman year,

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:19.220
we actually went through
some surveys and interviews

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:21.250
of students who self-disclosed

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.390
that they went to a high
school that was doing this

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:25.520
standards-based grading stuff,

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.830
they shared with us in our
research that was published

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:31.830
towards the end of 2020 in
the NASSP Bulletin Journal

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:35.387
that they had just an okay
time adjusting to the college

00:25:35.387 --> 00:25:36.670
and university level.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.840
And so if we have this idea in our mind

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:41.670
that it's somehow going to
wreck them as they transition

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:44.330
to the college university,
our research suggests

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:47.210
that's just not the case, as well.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:48.690
So thank you, Greg and others

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.640
for that wonderful, wonderful question.

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:52.573
- Excellent.

00:25:52.573 --> 00:25:54.990
Lots of good questions coming in.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:59.990
Here's another one thinking
about how does this apply

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:03.043
to gifted students?

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:05.786
- Fantastic, great, great, great.

00:26:05.786 --> 00:26:06.619
- And I wonder if there's thoughts about,

00:26:06.619 --> 00:26:09.980
yeah, does this create a ceiling that

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:11.580
prevents gifted students from maybe

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:14.010
moving into accelerated work or something?

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.538
- I think there's two facets
that are actually beneficial

00:26:16.538 --> 00:26:18.900
to gifted education.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:20.360
One of them is this.

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:23.260
I think about some students
I had in my own classroom

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:25.480
that were just high achievers

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:28.260
and I worked in a
high-achieving school district,

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:29.720
one of the most high-achieving
school districts

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:30.900
in the state of Iowa.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:34.370
And there were some students
who, they were still required,

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.310
this was pre standards-based grading.

00:26:36.310 --> 00:26:39.610
They were still required to do
the homework or the practice

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:41.820
because there was a point
value attached to it.

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:45.150
But they knew, their parents knew, I knew,

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:46.910
that they really didn't
need to do that practice,

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:49.280
and so it was just busy work for the sake

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:52.100
of getting points to get
the grade they wanted.

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:56.560
In a standards-based grading
philosophy or school,

00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:58.302
there is no point value attached

00:26:58.302 --> 00:27:00.530
to assignments that are intended to be

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:01.970
more practice-oriented.

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:03.320
So if I'm a gifted student,

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.400
now, I no longer am
subjected to doing busy work

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:08.980
for the sake of busy work to get points.

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:11.790
I think that's a huge win
for our gifted students

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:14.280
who may have other aspirations.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:17.307
Secondly, in some schools, what
they will do is they'll say,

00:27:17.307 --> 00:27:21.207
"Wow, this student based
upon this assessment

00:27:21.207 --> 00:27:23.437
"evidence I have right
now is already proficient.

00:27:23.437 --> 00:27:25.787
"They're already rocking and rolling

00:27:25.787 --> 00:27:28.167
"with a specific standard,
and so I'm going to provide

00:27:28.167 --> 00:27:32.500
"some level of extension
activity for this student."

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:34.780
Before we would not have had that same

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:38.490
detailed level of
information for the learner,

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:40.220
because prior to standards-based grading,

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:43.500
we would not have known by
the student, by the standard

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.640
who was our already getting it,

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.910
but with a standards-based
grading mentality and grade book

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:50.310
now we'll know specifically,

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.500
not just who the gifted students are,

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.080
but the specific standards

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:57.290
that they're absolutely
rocking and enrolling with.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:59.655
So Kristen, I think to summarize,

00:27:59.655 --> 00:28:03.760
this standards-based grading
thing can be very beneficial

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:07.870
to gifted students or to
high-achieving students,

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:11.340
because it provides
better information to them

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:14.040
and to their teachers
about where it is exactly

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:14.940
they're rocking and rolling.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:16.840
And it frankly just
eliminates some busy work

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:19.743
that they may have been
subjected to in the past.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:22.330
- Great, all right.

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:24.688
I'm gonna sneak in one more question here

00:28:24.688 --> 00:28:27.130
before we close out.

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:28.830
So the other place that
I've been hearing a lot

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:30.557
about standards-based
grading is in conversations

00:28:30.557 --> 00:28:34.240
around equity and in schools
that are serving students

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.470
from historically
under-resourced communities.

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:38.990
Tell me how you see standard-based grading

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:41.620
potentially helping those students.

00:28:41.620 --> 00:28:43.680
- Yeah, there's plenty of
research that has been done

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:47.120
about the factors that teachers consider

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:48.040
in determining a grade.

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:50.270
And this is again, pre
standards-based grading.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:51.650
And what we'll find in that research

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:55.343
is that often teachers consider
things like participation,

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:58.340
sometimes even attendance.

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:00.260
And what we know when we look at students

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:04.220
of different backgrounds
is that by grading

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.310
based upon participation and attendance,

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:11.310
that is a very inequitable
practice for some student groups.

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:13.860
I also think about, just to focus in

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:18.130
on a very specific practice
that I'm ashamed to say

00:29:18.130 --> 00:29:19.910
that I did in my own classroom,

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:21.810
that's awarding extra credit points

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:23.620
for students bringing in materials,

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:26.790
such as Kleenex boxes
or whiteboard markers.

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:29.007
Think about the families
that are able to do that

00:29:29.007 --> 00:29:31.630
and the families that
are not able to do that.

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:34.800
It's pretty obvious
that there are families

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:38.220
who are able to buy their kids' grades

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:39.720
or improve their kids' grades

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:42.120
by bringing in these materials.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.760
And I think of course,
this is just a far out

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.080
over-exaggerated example, but this happens

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.630
to the point where there
was a legislator in Iowa

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:52.670
several years ago that proposed a law

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:56.030
to eliminate this practice
because of the very question

00:29:56.030 --> 00:29:56.863
that you brought up, Kristen.

00:29:56.863 --> 00:29:59.780
And so perhaps the
viewers and listeners here

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.010
that are thinking maybe
just a real small step

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:05.260
they consider, if we are to eliminate

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:08.280
the awarding of extra
credit for materials,

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:11.910
that's one step closer to more
equitable grading practices.

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:14.800
And if we want to eliminate
providing some points

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:19.460
in our classrooms for
participation or for attendance,

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:21.560
that would be another
great and wonderful step

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:25.770
we could do that is more
equitable for students

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:27.610
of different backgrounds and demographics.

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:30.340
So, yeah, and I'm just gonna
shout out to Joe Feldman,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.890
who wrote a fantastic book
called "Grading for Equity"

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:35.370
that speaks even more
detail to this very topic,

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:37.940
and so we're hearing some
pretty big school districts

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:39.870
across the state saying we wanna make

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:41.140
some of these grading changes

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:45.000
because equity is our
driving force behind it.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.750
I'm saddened to see that
that COVID-19 happened,

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.800
but in a way I'm pretty excited to know

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:53.330
that as a result of COVID-19 happening,

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:57.260
that more schools are recognizing
how to create and utilize

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:58.760
more equitable grading practices.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:02.110
So thank you for bringing
that topic to light, Kristen.

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:03.250
- Absolutely.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.760
Well, this 30 minutes has flown by.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.400
It's been so nice talking to you.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:09.530
Thank you for all the
research you're doing

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:12.490
and the work you're doing
to help schools and teachers

00:31:12.490 --> 00:31:13.610
make this shift.

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:15.690
It's been a pleasure talking to you today.

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:17.390
- Thank you very much,
Kristen, for having me.

00:31:17.390 --> 00:31:18.763
It's been a pleasure.

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:22.030
- Great, and thanks for everyone out there

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:23.420
who's been listening.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:24.803
We will see you next time.

